.TH CLARUSC 1 2026-10-27 Clarus "Clarus Manual"
.SH NAME
clarusc \- the Clarus compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clarusc
.RB [ \-\-rtdir
.IR DIR ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc check
.RB [ \-\-rtdir
.IR DIR ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc build
.B \-o
.I PROG
.RB [ \-\-rtdir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-c ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc build68k
.B \-o
.I APP.bin
.RB [ \-\-rtdir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-testapi ]
.RB [ \-\-listing ]
.RB [ \-\-capture ]
.RB [ \-\-events
.IR EVFILE ]
.RB [ \-\-rtbake
.IR CLIR ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc emit
.B \-o
.I OUT.c
.RB [ \-\-rtdir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-testapi ]
.RB [ \-\-rtbake
.IR CLIR ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc appinfo
.RB [ \-\-testapi ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B clarusc \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clarusc
compiles Clarus, an event-driven language for System 6 and System 7
68k Macintosh computers.
It builds native 68k Mac applications directly, with no C compiler,
and it builds ordinary host executables by way of generated C.
.PP
Every
.I FILE
is a Clarus source file; together they form one program, and each
file's
.B include
directives are followed.
Diagnostics print to standard output as
.IR path : line : col :\ message ,
all parse errors before all type errors.
The runtime modules a program needs are found in the runtime directory
(see
.BR FILES )
and added automatically.
.SH COMMANDS
The command is the first argument.
Without one,
.B clarusc
checks only.
.TP
.B check
Type-check the program and print its diagnostics.
Nothing is written.
.TP
.B build
Build a host executable,
.IR PROG .
The C translation of the program is written to a temporary file, then
the C compiler is run directly, never through a shell, with the
arguments
.RS
.I $CC $CFLAGS
.B \-I
.IB rtdir /../host
.B \-o
.I PROG tmp.c
.IB rtdir /../host/rt.c
.PP
where
.I rtdir
is the runtime directory.
The temporary file is removed afterwards unless
.B \-\-keep\-c
is given.
.RE
.TP
.B build68k
Build a native 68k Mac application,
.IR APP.bin ,
as MacBinary.
No C compiler is used.
Progress is logged to standard error.
.TP
.B emit
Write the C translation of the program to
.I OUT.c
and stop.
.TP
.B appinfo
Check the program and print its application metadata as
.IB key = value
lines:
.B app=1
(when the program has an
.B app
section),
.BR name= ,
and
.BR version= ,
.BR id= ,
.B icon=
when set.
.TP
.B emit68k
A deprecated alias for
.BR build68k .
It prints
.B clarusc: 'emit68k' is deprecated; please use 'build68k'
on standard error, then does the same.
.PP
A bare
.B clarusc
.I FILE ...
is
.B check
plus the line
.B clarusc: checking only; use 'clarusc build' or 'clarusc build68k' to produce an executable
on standard error.
.B \-o
without a command does not build.
.PP
.B \-\-bake\-ir
as the first argument builds the runtime library ClarusC.APPL
carries; it is internal build tooling.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o \ PATH
The output file.
Required by
.BR build ,
.B build68k
and
.BR emit .
.TP
.BI \-\-rtdir \ DIR
Use
.I DIR
as the runtime directory, the one holding the runtime modules (its path
normally ends in
.IR runtime/clarus/ ),
skipping the search described in
.BR FILES .
Accepted by every command except
.BR appinfo .
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-c
.RB ( build )
Keep the temporary C file and print its path on standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-testapi
.RB ( build68k ,
.BR emit ,
.BR appinfo )
For a UI program, make the UI runtime and its test hooks visible to the
program.
Used by test suites; it has no effect on a program without a UI.
.TP
.B \-\-listing
.RB ( build68k )
Also write, for each code segment
.IR N ,
an assembly listing
.IB base .seg N .s
and the segment's raw bytes
.IB base .seg N .dat\c
, where
.I base
is the
.B \-o
path without its extension.
.TP
.B \-\-capture
.RB ( build68k )
Build with the test capture protocol: the application writes an
.B out
file with its alerts, its UI trace and an exit trailer.
Without
.B \-\-capture
or
.B \-\-events
the build is a production build and writes no such file.
.TP
.BI \-\-events \ EVFILE
.RB ( build68k )
Script the application's input from
.I EVFILE
for deterministic UI testing.
Implies
.BR \-\-capture .
.TP
.BI \-\-rtbake \ CLIR
.RB ( build68k ,
.BR emit )
Take the runtime from the prebuilt library
.I CLIR
instead of compiling it from source.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.B clarusc
.I version
and exit, wherever it appears on the command line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLARUS_DATADIR
A share directory, such as
.IR /usr/local/share/clarus ,
whose
.I runtime/clarus/
is tried before the built-in default (see
.BR FILES ).
Keep it under about 200 bytes; a longer one is passed over.
.TP
.B CC
.RB ( build )
The C compiler.
When set and non-empty it replaces the compiler chosen when
.B clarusc
was built (normally
.BR cc ).
It is split on spaces and tabs: the first word is the program, the rest
are its leading arguments.
.TP
.B CFLAGS
.RB ( build )
When set, even to the empty string, its words (split on spaces and
tabs, with no quote processing) replace the default
.BR \-O1 .
.TP
.B TMPDIR
.RB ( build )
The directory for the temporary C file, else
.IR /tmp .
.SH FILES
With
.BI \-\-rtdir \ DIR\c
,
.I DIR
is used as is, with no fallback.
Otherwise the runtime directory is the first of these whose files are
actually there:
.IP 1. 4
a
.I runtime/clarus/
directory in the current directory or up to nine parent directories;
.IP 2. 4
.IR $CLARUS_DATADIR /runtime/clarus/\c
;
.IP 3. 4
.IR /usr/local/share/clarus/runtime/clarus/ ,
the built-in default.
The data directory is set when
.B clarusc
is built
.RB ( "make install DATADIR=" ...);
a development build has none.
.PP
An
.B include
path not found relative to the including file is looked up in the
.I toolbox/
directory beside the runtime's
.I runtime/
directory,
.IR rtdir /../../toolbox/.
.PP
An installed layout, with the default
.BR PREFIX :
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/clarusc
the compiler
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/clarus/runtime/clarus/
the Clarus runtime modules
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/clarus/runtime/host/
the host C runtime
.RI ( rt.c ,
.IR rt*.h ,
.IR rt*.inc )
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/clarus/toolbox/
the Toolbox declaration catalog
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/doc/clarus/
the language reference, changelog and Toolbox cookbook
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/clarusc.1
this page
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The program has errors, an input or output file could not be read or
written, or
.B build
failed (including a C compiler that exited non-zero).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.TP
.B 3
.B clarusc
itself hit an internal runtime error (it prints
.BR "runtime error:" \ ...),
such as running out of memory; otherwise a compiler bug.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a program:
.PP
.RS
clarusc check hello.cla
.RE
.PP
Build and run a host executable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
clarusc build \-o hello hello.cla
\&./hello
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a Mac application:
.PP
.RS
clarusc build68k \-o Hello.bin hello\-mac.cla
.RE
.PP
Build with a different C compiler and flags:
.PP
.RS
CC=clang CFLAGS="\-O2 \-g" clarusc build \-o hello hello.cla
.RE
.PP
Use an uninstalled checkout's runtime:
.PP
.RS
clarusc build \-\-rtdir ~/clarus/runtime/clarus/ \-o hello hello.cla
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cc (1)
.PP
The installed documentation, in
.I /usr/local/share/doc/clarus/
by default:
.IR clarus\-language\-reference.md ,
the normative language reference;
.IR clarus\-language\-changelog.md ;
and
.IR clarus\-toolbox\-cookbook.md ,
on declaring Inside Macintosh routines in Clarus.
